</ol>


</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/13.E:_Fundamental_Equilibrium_Concepts_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_65"><span id="Q13.3.10"></span><h3 id="Q13.3.10-44118">Q13.3.10</h3>


<p>Water gas is a 1:1 mixture of carbon monoxide and hydrogen gas and is called water gas because it is formed from steam and hot carbon in the following reaction:</p>

<p>\[\ce{H2O}(g)+\ce{C}(s)\rightleftharpoons\ce{H2}(g)+\ce{CO}(g).\]</p>

<p>Methanol, a liquid fuel that could possibly replace gasoline, can be prepared from water gas and hydrogen at high temperature and pressure in the presence of a suitable catalyst.</p>

<ol start="1" style="list-style-type: lower-alpha;">
    <li>Write the expression for the equilibrium constant (\(K_c\)) for the reversible reaction \[\ce{2H2}(g)+\ce{CO}(g)\rightleftharpoons\ce{CH3OH}(g) \hspace{20px} &Delta;H=\mathrm{-90.2\:kJ}\]</li>
    <li>What will happen to the concentrations of \(\ce{H2}\), \(\ce{CO}\), and \(\ce{CH3OH}\) at equilibrium if more H<sub>2</sub> is added?</li>
    <li>What will happen to the concentrations of H\(\ce{H2}\), \(\ce{CO}\), and \(\ce{CH3OH}\) at equilibrium if CO is removed?</li>
    <li>What will happen to the concentrations of \(\ce{H2}\), \(\ce{CO}\), and \(\ce{CH3OH}\) at equilibrium if CH<sub>3</sub>OH is added?</li>
    <li>What will happen to the concentrations of H\(\ce{H2}\), \(\ce{CO}\), and \(\ce{CH3OH}\) at equilibrium if the temperature of the system is increased?</li>
    <li>What will happen to the concentrations of \(\ce{H2}\), \(\ce{CO}\), and \(\ce{CH3OH}\) at equilibrium if more catalyst is added?</li>
</ol>


</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/13.E:_Fundamental_Equilibrium_Concepts_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_66"><span id="S13.3.10"></span><h3 id="S13.3.10-44118">S13.3.10</h3>


<ol start="1" style="list-style-type: lower-alpha;">
    <li>\(K_c=\ce{\dfrac{[CH3OH]}{[H2]^2[CO]}}\);</li>
    <li>[H<sub>2</sub>] increases, [CO] decreases, [CH<sub>3</sub>OH] increases;</li>
    <li>[H<sub>2</sub>] increases, [CO] decreases, [CH<sub>3</sub>OH] decreases;</li>
    <li>[H<sub>2</sub>] increases, [CO] increases, [CH<sub>3</sub>OH] increases;</li>
    <li>[H<sub>2</sub>] increases, [CO] increases, [CH<sub>3</sub>OH] decreases;</li>
    <li>no changes.</li>
</ol>


</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/13.E:_Fundamental_Equilibrium_Concepts_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_67"><span id="Q13.3.11"></span><h3 id="Q13.3.11-44118">Q13.3.11</h3>


<p>Nitrogen and oxygen react at high temperatures.</p>

<ol start="1" style="list-style-type: lower-alpha;">
