</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/13.E:_Fundamental_Equilibrium_Concepts_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_124"><span id="S13.4.22"></span><h3 id="S13.4.22-44118">S13.4.22</h3>

<ul>
    <li>[NO<sub>2</sub>] = 0.19 <em>M</em></li>
    <li><span>[NO] = 0.0070 </span><em>M</em></li>
    <li><span>[O</span><sub>2</sub><span>] = 0.0035 </span><em>M</em></li>
</ul>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/13.E:_Fundamental_Equilibrium_Concepts_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_125"><span id="Q13.4.23"></span><h3 id="Q13.4.23-44118">Q13.4.23</h3>

<p>Calculate the equilibrium concentrations that result when 0.25 <em>M</em> O<sub>2</sub> and 1.0 <em>M</em> HCl react and come to equilibrium.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center">\[\ce{4HCl}(g)+\ce{O2}(g)&#8652;\ce{2Cl2}(g)+\ce{2H2O}(g) \hspace{20px} K_c=3.1&times;10^{13}\]</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/13.E:_Fundamental_Equilibrium_Concepts_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_126"><span id="Q13.4.24"></span><h3 id="Q13.4.24-44118">Q13.4.24</h3>

<p>One of the important reactions in the formation of smog is represented by the equation</p>

<p class="mt-align-center">\[\ce{O3}(g)+\ce{NO}(g)&#8652;\ce{NO2}(g)+\ce{O2}(g) \hspace{20px} K_P=6.0&times;10^{34}\]</p>

<p>What is the pressure of O<sub>3</sub> remaining after a mixture of O<sub>3</sub> with a pressure of 1.2 &times; 10<sup>&minus;8</sup> atm and NO with a pressure of 1.2 &times; 10<sup>&minus;8</sup> atm comes to equilibrium? (Hint: <em>K<sub>P</sub></em> is large; assume the reaction goes to completion then comes back to equilibrium.)</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/13.E:_Fundamental_Equilibrium_Concepts_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_127"><span id="S13.4.24"></span><h3 id="S13.4.24-44118">S13.4.24</h3>

<p>\(P_{\ce{O3}}=4.9&times;10^{&minus;26}\:\ce{atm}\)</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/13.E:_Fundamental_Equilibrium_Concepts_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_128"><span id="Q13.4.24_2"></span><h3 id="Q13.4.24-44118">Q13.4.24</h3>

<p>Calculate the pressures of NO, Cl<sub>2</sub>, and NOCl in an equilibrium mixture produced by the reaction of a starting mixture with 4.0 atm NO and 2.0 atm Cl<sub>2</sub>. (Hint: <em>K<sub>P</sub></em> is small; assume the reverse reaction goes to completion then comes back to equilibrium.)</p>

